Jakob Creutzig wrote:
> No-one has any right to demand that the DevTeam should give
> up what is entirely theirs, or to release something, or
> to fix a bug, or to respond to e-mails.  And that's not
> because they worked so hard to please us, but simply
> because there's no constituted duty automatically coming
> in effect just because someone wrote software other
> people are using.

These things are true:

- The Dev Team doesn't have to supply anything.
- The Dev Team doesn't have to fix bugs.
- The Dev Team doesn't have to talk to anyone.
- The Dev Team doesn't have to release new versions.
- The Dev Team doesn't have to release the source code.

But...

- People don't have to play Nethack.

That's why there have been so many variants over Nethack's long
history: NH+, NH--, NH:tng, Slash, Slash'em, Wizard patch, Lethe, Heck
and more.  The length of time between Nethack releases, in my opinion,
are a possible reason that ADOM became popular.  (And notice that it's
also been lacking in releases lately.)

Nethack is a game that contains a great many things... but eventually,
players reach a point where they say to themselves, "Wait... is that
all?"

To give my own experience as an example, I played the game for years
before I got my first ascension.  Now, over at nethack.alt.org, I've
ascended probably three of my last ten games, one of them a tourist.
Once you've learn all the many tricks and strategems, the luster sort
of begins to come off of the venerable game, and you start looking for
new versions.  (Or you start playing conducts.)  The patched versions
(when they don't do things like add assault rifiles) may help to
allieviate this, but may not.

But back to vanilla, I wonder how many features have been rejected from
the core game because it would make it unable to run on all the many
systems Nethack supports?  The game still runs on Amigas and Atari STs,
does keeping the game able to fit within memory hinder what they can
add to the game?

Maybe what we need is for someone to create an "alternate" game, one
that discards compatibility with older computers yet exercises greater
editorial control over what makes it into the game?  (Note: Of course,
many of Slash'em's features can be disabled at compile-time.  Maybe
that would be a good place to start?)

